    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable that you are questioning this. When an all-male panel selects a male candidate whose leadership credentials appear on paper to be less extensive than yours, it naturally raises concerns about fairness.\n\nHere is an analysis of what an all-male panel signifies and how you should approach it:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is it a sign of anything?\n\n* **Risk of In-group / Affinity Bias:** Research across organizational psychology consistently shows that homogenous panels are more vulnerable to similarity bias (unconsciously favoring candidates with whom panel members naturally identify or relate to socially and culturally).\n* **Weak Recruitment Governance:** In secondary education and public sector recruitment, best practice guidelines strongly recommend—and in many jurisdictions or school districts formally require—gender diversity on senior leadership panels. A panel of four men suggests a lack of attention to modern equitable hiring practices.\n* **Context, Not Conclusive Proof:** Legally and procedurally, an all-male panel alone is rarely sufficient evidence of discrimination. However, it can serve as a **supporting indicator** when combined with objective disparities in qualifications and a lack of procedural transparency.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Qualification Disparity is Your Stronger Point\n\nThe most significant factor here is not just who was in the room, but **how the candidates were scored against the job specification**:\n* You have a **Master’s in Educational Leadership** and **5 years of direct senior management experience** as a deputy principal.\n* The selected candidate has **courses in leadership** and **classroom teaching experience**, but apparently lacks direct vice/deputy principal experience.\n\nUnless the interview performance or vision presentation was weighted overwhelmingly above administrative qualifications, this creates a clear basis to ask how the essential and desirable criteria were evaluated.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should you \"make something\" of the panel composition?\n\n**Do not lead with the panel's gender composition outright**, but **do keep it as documented context.**\n\nIf your opening move is to accuse the process of gender bias based purely on the panel's demographic makeup, the institution will likely become defensive and dismiss the claim as speculation. Instead, focus on **transparency, scoring rubrics, and objective criteria**.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Follow Up on the Written Explanation:**\n   * Send a polite, formal follow-up noting the date of your initial request (two weeks ago).\n   * Specifically request:\n     * Your individual scores and feedback against the advertised essential/desirable criteria.\n     * The scoring matrix or rubric used by the panel.\n     * Clarification on how direct administrative experience vs. teaching experience was weighted.\n\n2. **Consult Your Union or Professional Association:**\n   * If you are a member of an educational leadership union or professional association, contact their legal/employment advisory service immediately.\n   * They can inform you of:\n     * Any statutory requirements in your jurisdiction regarding written feedback timelines.\n     * Policies mandating balanced recruitment panels in your school district or governing body.\n     * Precedents for formal appeals or equal-opportunity inquiries.\n\n3. **Check Local Freedom of Information / Access to Records Laws:**\n   * Depending on your country/state and whether the school is public, you may have legal rights to view panel notes, candidate scoring sheets (often anonymized for other candidates), and panel appointment protocols.\n\n4. **Keep Meticulous Records:**\n   * Document all dates, interview questions you recall, names of panel members, the job specification, and copies of all correspondence. If the written explanation reveals procedural flaws or arbitrary scoring, the composition of the panel becomes relevant context 